WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:12.120
Today we are going to see some amazing, and some&nbsp;
less amazing results from Stable Diffusion XL,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:17.880
the popular text to image AI’s new version&nbsp;
that you can also run for free online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.600
or even at home. So, what’s new here?

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.840
Well, one it offers higher resolution&nbsp;
images and is better at challenging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:33.540
concepts that previous text to&nbsp;
image AIs faltered at. Human hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:38.820
very specific spatial arrangements where we&nbsp;
say that a woman should be in the background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:45.420
chasing a dog in the foreground. These now&nbsp;
work better. However, I’ll immediately say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.660
that this does not mean perfect. Here are some&nbsp;
of the results I generated locally. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:59.940
it cannot be anything else but images of Scholars&nbsp;
holding on to their papers, or at least studying a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:06.780
huge magical tome. It can do that, however,&nbsp;
look. Hands still seem to be an issue.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:13.740
If you have a favorite artist, and you love&nbsp;
the style, but would like to imagine what if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:20.640
he explored painting different subjects, you&nbsp;
can now do it at home, for free. I think this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:28.620
is an amazing tool to explore new artistic ideas,&nbsp;
and I have to say it is incredibly fun too. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:33.540
when comparing to Midjourney, I think the&nbsp;
quality of the results are better there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:41.040
however, SDXL seems to be more true to the&nbsp;
original style of the artist. Loving it.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.320
I could also not resist trying Danielle&nbsp;
Baskin’s amazing drink prompts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.320
which are also working quite well. Yummy.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.000
Now, they say that users generally&nbsp;
prefer the new technique’s results&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.340
to previous versions of Stable Diffusion,&nbsp;
although I haven’t seen this user study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.740
being linked to a peer-reviewed paper,&nbsp;
so a wise Fellow Scholar like you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.540
does not take these results for granted.&nbsp;
We are going to try some more ourselves.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:15.000
Two, simpler prompting. If you have played&nbsp;
with previous versions of Stable Diffusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.220
you probably noticed that it requires a very&nbsp;
detailed description of an image to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:26.820
something decent. But here, it is easier to&nbsp;
create something with just a few words. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:33.480
experiments have mostly matched this. When asking&nbsp;
for illustration of a small modern house in Osaka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:39.120
I got quite usable images that I liked.&nbsp;
This layered cake in the style of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:45.660
landscape was also just a couple words, and&nbsp;
the results are incredible. This is so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:51.420
fun! I hope you Fellow Scholars will also&nbsp;
come up with some crazy ideas using this.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:59.040
Three, it finally supports better text generation.&nbsp;
Now, writing text is typically quite difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:05.520
for text to image AIs. When I asked for the full&nbsp;
“Two Minute Papers” in writing, I did get better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:11.520
results than with most previous techniques&nbsp;
I’ve used, but most of the time, this was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:18.180
little too much to ask for. After many tries,&nbsp;
I had some success with it. Kind of. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:25.020
I found that when asking for just “SDXL”,&nbsp;
even then it took a bit of trial end error,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:33.120
but I finally started seeing some more success.&nbsp;
We are not there yet, but this is just the 1.0&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:39.480
version of Stable Diffusion XL, so I am&nbsp;
excited to see how it improves over time.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:46.140
Also do you remember ControlNet? ControlNet is&nbsp;
a neural network structure that helps us give it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:52.800
additional inputs beyond just text to image.
And it is absolutely amazing. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:59.280
we can provide just the edges of an input&nbsp;
image. A rough sketch, or edges extracted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:05.880
from a real photo, and we get a beautiful image&nbsp;
with exactly that creature and framing. Other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:11.520
kinds of inputs also work with ControlNet,&nbsp;
and as the preliminary results show, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:18.000
feature is coming soon to Stable Diffusion XL,&nbsp;
which will bump up its usability significantly.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:26.100
And we get all of these for free, forever. That&nbsp;
is excellent. It is all so new and so fresh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:31.080
there aren’t too many results out there&nbsp;
yet. And don’t forget, there are many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:37.320
ways to improve this base model through&nbsp;
checkpoints and magical things called LoRAs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:43.020
so specialized versions of SDXL&nbsp;
will come out as soon as weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:49.320
or perhaps days from now. It will be so&nbsp;
much better. What a time to be alive!

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:55.440
And if you wish to try it in your browser&nbsp;
or run it locally, I got you covered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:00.600
some links in the video description.&nbsp;
So for now, let the experiments begin!

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.600
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

